@startuml mcp_tier_loading
' Tiered MCP Loading — backs ADR-003.
' State diagram of how the context budget pool gains MCP tools over a session.

title Tiered MCP Loading

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Roboto

[*] --> SessionStart

state "Session Start" as SessionStart {
  SessionStart : Load Tier 1 tools\n(always-on minimum set)
  SessionStart : Context budget: base
}

state "Tier 1 Loaded" as T1 {
  T1 : Tools available: core MCP set
  T1 : Budget used: ~5-10%
}

state "Tier 2 Bundled" as T2 {
  T2 : Tools available: T1 + agent bundle
  T2 : Budget used: ~20-40%
}

state "Tier 3 Activated" as T3 {
  T3 : Tools available: T1 + T2 + matched
  T3 : Budget used: up to ~60%
}

SessionStart --> T1 : SessionStart\nhooks complete

T1 --> T2 : Agent invoked\n(agent's Tier 2 bundle loads)
T1 --> T3 : Keyword match\n(Tier 3 tool triggered)

T2 --> T3 : Additional keyword\nmatch during agent call
T3 --> T2 : Agent returns\n(T3 tools stay loaded)

T2 --> T1 : Agent returns\n(T2 unloads)

state "Usage Tracked" as Tracked
T1 --> Tracked : every turn
T2 --> Tracked : every turn
T3 --> Tracked : every turn
Tracked : scripts/track-mcp-usage.sh
Tracked : records which tools fired
Tracked --> T1 : telemetry written

note right of SessionStart
  The tier split is defined in
  .claude/rules/mcp-strategy.md.
  Loading all tools unconditionally
  would consume 85-95% of the
  context budget before the model
  sees the user's first prompt.
end note

note right of Tracked
  Usage telemetry informs tier
  promotion decisions: a heavily
  used Tier 3 tool may graduate
  to Tier 2, and so on.
end note

@enduml
